1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,560
Atomics help when the problem is updating
a value. Many concurrent designs are not
about a value at all — they are about
timing. Three workers must finish warming
caches before the server accepts traffic.

2
00:00:11,679 --> 00:00:23,079
A parallel algorithm must wait until every
thread reaches the end of a phase before
any starts the next. At most five database
connections may be checked out at once.
Those are coordination problems.

3
00:00:23,199 --> 00:00:36,439
Reinventing them with bare wait and notify
is possible and usually unwise. Latches,
barriers, and semaphores are the standard
synchronizers for those shapes. Pick the
simplest tool that matches the phase you
mean.

4
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:48,640
CountDownLatch is a one-shot gate. You set
a count. Workers call countDown when they
finish a step. Waiters call await until
the count reaches zero. Look at this java
for a moment. Three workers start.

5
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:58,080
Each counts down once when ready. The
coordinator blocks in await until all
three have counted down. Then it opens the
gate and continues. The latch does not
reset.

6
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:10,719
That one-shot nature is a feature when
startup or a single rendezvous is the
point. Forgetting to countDown on an error
path is the classic hang: one worker
throws, never counts down, and await waits
forever.

7
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:20,400
Use try/finally so the count moves even
when work fails — or fail the whole
startup explicitly instead of stalling.
CyclicBarrier is for recurring phases.

8
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:34,040
Parties arrive, wait until all are
present, then the barrier releases and can
be reused for the next round: Look at this
java for a moment. Four workers finish
phase one, meet at await, then start phase
two together.

9
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:45,920
Latch versus barrier is the practical
contrast: one-shot event set versus
reusable multi-phase rendezvous. Semaphore
manages permits. Acquire a permit to
enter; release when done.

10
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:54,560
A semaphore of five is a clean way to
limit concurrent access to a scarce
resource without hand-rolling a counter
and condition pair: Look at this java for
a moment.

11
00:01:54,680 --> 00:02:04,960
Leaking permits — acquiring and forgetting
to release on exceptions — slowly starves
the system. The finally release is the
same ownership instinct as unlocking a
ReentrantLock.

12
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:15,240
What if we skip these utilities and write
our own wait/notify protocol for every
gate? You can, and you will relearn missed
signals, spurious wakes, and forgotten
notifies.

13
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:22,760
Prefer the named tool whose contract
matches the story you are telling in the
code review. A teaching contrast locks the
vocabulary.

14
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:31,600
Use a latch when the story is "wait until
N one-time events happen." Use a barrier
when the story is "N parties meet at the
end of each phase,

15
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:45,120
then repeat." Use a semaphore when the
story is "at most N may enter." If you
catch yourself explaining a custom
wait/notify graph that matches one of
those sentences, delete the custom graph
and use the synchronizer.

16
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:53,760
Error paths deserve one more beat. A
worker that fails before countDown is
indistinguishable from a worker that never
existed — to the waiter.

17
00:02:53,880 --> 00:03:05,000
Decide whether failure counts down and
records an error, or whether failure
aborts the latch by other means. Silence
is the worst policy; hangs are how silence
presents itself.

18
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:13,920
Picture three initializer threads warming
cache, connecting to a broker, and loading
feature flags. A latch of three gates the
HTTP acceptor.

19
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:25,400
If flag loading fails, count down anyway
and set a failed state the acceptor checks
— or refuse to open the gate and crash the
process loudly. Either policy beats a
silent hang.

20
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:30,920
Name the synchronizer after the phase:
startupLatch, phaseBarrier, dbPermits.

21
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:40,840
Clear names prevent the next developer
from using a latch where a barrier
belonged simply because both "wait for
others." Once producers create work faster
than consumers can take it,

22
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:47,520
another coordination structure dominates:
a queue that blocks, bounds memory, and
carries jobs between stages.
